From: Alessio Ferri <alessio.ferri@mythread.it>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] b43: measure the background noise on N-PHY

Both halves of the link quality calculation return early unless the PHY is
a G, so on an N-PHY dev->stats.link_noise keeps the value it was
initialised with and mac80211 is handed a constant as the noise floor.

The N-PHY reports through the power indication block rather than through
JSSI: the ucode leaves one 32-bit complex power value per core in shared
memory, and brcmsmac turns it into dBm by scaling it down by the number of
samples the ucode averaged, converting to dB, and adding the fixed offset
of this chip family. Do the same, and clear the block before asking for a
sample so a stale one cannot be read as fresh.

Verified against an MMIO capture of the proprietary wl driver 6.30.102.7 on
a BCM6362: it reads the same four shared memory words, and running its
sampled values through this computation gives noise floors between -82 and
-88 dBm, per core.

The mechanism is per PHY type, not per revision: brcmsmac uses this same
path for every N-PHY revision, and the capture only covers rev 8. The
change is still bounded for the untested ones, since what they report today
is a value that is never measured at all.

Signed-off-by: Alessio Ferri <alessio.ferri@mythread.it>
---
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/b43.h
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/b43.h
@@ -335,6 +335,11 @@
 #define B43_SHM_SH_NPHY_TXIQW2		0x0704
 #define B43_SHM_SH_NPHY_TXIQW3		0x0706
 /* SHM_SHARED tx pwr ctrl */
+/* Power indication block: the ucode leaves one 32-bit complex power value per
+ * core here, low half first. Same offsets brcmsmac calls M_PWRIND_BLKS.
+ */
+#define B43_SHM_SH_PWRIND_BLKS		0x0308
+#define B43_SHM_SH_PWRIND(i)		(B43_SHM_SH_PWRIND_BLKS + 2 * (i))
 #define B43_SHM_SH_NPHY_TXPWR_INDX0	0x0708
 #define B43_SHM_SH_NPHY_TXPWR_INDX1	0x070E
 
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/main.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/main.c
@@ -1397,16 +1397,65 @@
 
 static void b43_generate_noise_sample(struct b43_wldev *dev)
 {
-	b43_jssi_write(dev, 0x7F7F7F7F);
+	int i;
+
+	if (dev->phy.type == B43_PHYTYPE_N) {
+		/* The ucode reports through the power indication block on this
+		 * PHY, not through JSSI. Clear it so a stale sample cannot be
+		 * mistaken for a fresh one.
+		 */
+		for (i = 0; i < 4; i++)
+			b43_shm_write16(dev, B43_SHM_SHARED,
+					B43_SHM_SH_PWRIND(i), 0);
+	} else {
+		b43_jssi_write(dev, 0x7F7F7F7F);
+	}
 	b43_write32(dev, B43_MMIO_MACCMD,
 		    b43_read32(dev, B43_MMIO_MACCMD) | B43_MACCMD_BGNOISE);
 }
 
+/* The ucode reports the measured complex power per core in the power indication
+ * block. Turn it into a noise floor the way brcmsmac does for this PHY: one
+ * 32-bit value per core, scaled down by the number of samples the ucode
+ * averaged, converted to dB and shifted by the fixed offset of the family.
+ */
+static void b43_nphy_noise_sample(struct b43_wldev *dev)
+{
+	s8 noise = -92;
+	int core;
+
+	for (core = 0; core < 2; core++) {
+		u32 pwr;
+		u8 msb, secondmsb = 0;
+		s8 dbm;
+
+		pwr = b43_shm_read16(dev, B43_SHM_SHARED,
+				     B43_SHM_SH_PWRIND(2 * core));
+		pwr |= (u32)b43_shm_read16(dev, B43_SHM_SHARED,
+					   B43_SHM_SH_PWRIND(2 * core + 1)) << 16;
+		if (!pwr)
+			continue;
+
+		pwr >>= 9;
+		msb = fls(pwr);
+		if (msb) {
+			msb--;
+			if (msb)
+				secondmsb = (pwr >> (msb - 1)) & 1;
+		}
+		dbm = 3 * msb + 2 * secondmsb - 103;
+		if (dbm > noise)
+			noise = dbm;
+	}
+
+	dev->stats.link_noise = noise;
+}
+
 static void b43_calculate_link_quality(struct b43_wldev *dev)
 {
 	/* Top half of Link Quality calculation. */
 
-	if (dev->phy.type != B43_PHYTYPE_G)
+	if (dev->phy.type != B43_PHYTYPE_G && dev->phy.type != B43_PHYTYPE_N)
 		return;
 	if (dev->noisecalc.calculation_running)
 		return;
@@ -1426,6 +1475,12 @@
 
 	/* Bottom half of Link Quality calculation. */
 
+	if (dev->phy.type == B43_PHYTYPE_N) {
+		b43_nphy_noise_sample(dev);
+		dev->noisecalc.calculation_running = false;
+		return;
+	}
+
 	if (dev->phy.type != B43_PHYTYPE_G)
 		return;
 

Note per il repo, non per la mailing list:

  - Come e' venuta fuori: da reverse-tools/phy_type_audit.py, che elenca i
    costrutti dove b43 cita la PHY G e non la N. handle_irq_noise e
    b43_calculate_link_quality erano due delle venti voci.
  - Meccanismo, da brcmsmac (in-tree, GPL): wlc_phy_noise_sample_request nel
    ramo ISNPHY azzera M_PWRIND_MAP0..3 e alza MCMD_BG_NOISE;
    wlc_phy_noise_sample_intr legge le quattro parole, compone
    (hi << 16) | lo per core, divide per 512 (PHY_NOISE_SAMPLE_LOG_NUM_UCODE),
    converte con wlc_phy_compute_dB e somma PHY_NOISE_OFFSETFACT_4322 = -103.
    Il rumore riportato e' il massimo fra i core.
  - Gli offset: M_PWRIND_BLKS = 0x184 * 2 = 0x308, e la cattura legge
    esattamente 0x308, 0x30a, 0x30c, 0x30e, 96 volte. Qui le due codifiche
    coincidono, a differenza del blocco TXPWR_INDX (vedi test/README.md).
  - Verifica sui dati: presi i 96 valori dai RETVAL della cattura e passati per
    questa conversione, escono -82, -86, -88 dBm per core. Plausibili e
    coerenti fra i due core, che e' quanto si puo' dire senza uno strumento.
  - Cosa NON fa: la finestra di 8 campioni che brcmsmac tiene per core. Qui il
    campione corrente diventa il valore riportato, e la media nel tempo la
    fanno le richieste periodiche. Se serve una finestra, si aggiunge dopo
    aver visto se cambia qualcosa in pratica.
  - Il gate e' sul TIPO di PHY e non sulla revisione: e' l'asse giusto per
    questa cosa, ma tocca tutte le N-PHY e non solo il rev 8. E' l'unica delle
    sei patch che esce dal recinto del nostro radio, e va detto a chi rivede.
  - NON provata su hardware.
